fix(recall): admit semantic-only candidates; stop measuring a D4 index

The inverted text index was acting as a gate rather than a prefilter in
CLI recall: blocks absent from the lexical candidate list were skipped, and
an empty lexical set aborted the whole depth scan. Vector hits were
therefore only ranked when they also happened to be lexical hits, and a
paraphrase sharing no token returned nothing at all -- precisely the case
the embedding path exists to serve.

Merge both candidate sources into one sorted, deduplicated list
(relevance::merge_candidates) and rank only those. The depth window became
redundant once candidates come from the full index and was removed.
Out-of-range ids are dropped centrally. Four regression tests cover the
no-lexical-overlap case, the union, and corrupt-index ids.

Also:

- eval_real.sh no longer rewrites [embedding].max_depth from 5 to 4, so
  the committed configuration reproduces the published number. It now
  parses the config it generates and aborts unless provider, model,
  semantic_weight and max_depth are as expected, because a malformed TOML
  makes the binary fall back to defaults silently. The build no longer
  runs through grep | head, which discarded its exit status. The hardcoded
  temp path became MM_ROOT.

- tests appended to the real layers/session.txt on every run: the store
  path resolves layers_dir/<layer>.txt and the default is ./layers
  relative to the CWD, which is the repository root under cargo test. The
  test config now redirects layers_dir, and the polluted corpus file is
  restored.

- .gitattributes had no *.sh rule, so eval_real.sh could come back as CRLF;
  bash then reads the terminator as a bare "fi" plus CR and fails to parse.

WHITEPAPER 10.1 and BENCHMARKS are reconciled: the 75.0% R@5 is labelled
as a D4, pre-fix measurement, the FAISS 96.7% as a diagnostic ceiling over
60 fact vectors rather than a like-for-like baseline, and the D5 run and
the effect of this fix are both marked unmeasured. Test counts 413 -> 417.

